{#
    A frame, and — where it is a specimen — the caption that says what it is.

    Two nodes share this template because they are the same thing: a document
    of somebody else's, shown inside this one. A video has a title and nothing
    else to say; a specimen names the card and the size it was measured at,
    because that number is checked by `make fit` and a reader who wants to know
    what they are looking at should not have to open the source.

    **The element, not a bare `<iframe>`.** What the two need is a frame that
    is sized, bordered and captioned the way every other block on the page is,
    and `sds-embed` is where that is decided once — see its own file for the
    two shapes and why an embed has to state which one it is. Left to the
    renderer this was a raw frame with the browser's 1996 ridge around it, as
    wide as its `width` attribute said and running straight out of any column
    narrower than that.

    The frame itself is still written here, between the tags, and the element
    lifts it rather than writing a second one. That is the rule the whole theme
    is built on: the server writes, the element upgrades. A reader with no
    JavaScript has the video and the card — framed by `document.css`, which
    holds exactly this window — and nothing about the page waits on a script
    to show its evidence.

    Not lazy, for the same reason: a specimen is the evidence on the page, and
    a frame that loads on scroll is a frame that is blank in every screenshot
    the gate takes — which is the one place somebody looks at all of them at
    once.
#}
{%- if node.hasOption('specimen') -%}
{#- Fixed. The card declares the viewport it was measured at and this is that
    number, so the frame keeps it and scrolls where the column is narrower;
    a card reflowed to fit would be documenting a layout nothing checked. -#}
<sds-embed
    {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
    ><iframe src="{{ asset(node.url) }}"
        {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
        {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif %}
        title="{{ node.option('title')|default('Specimen') }}"></iframe
    ><div class="sds-embed__caption">{{- node.option('title')|default('Specimen') }} · <span class="sds-mono">{{ node.option('viewport') }}</span></div></sds-embed
>
{%- else -%}
{#- Fluid. A player's `width` and `height` are its native size and not the
    size it wants: it fills the column it is given and holds the shape those
    two state, which is the one thing about it that must not change. -#}
<sds-embed ratio="{{ node.option('width')|default(560) }} / {{ node.option('height')|default(315) }}"
    ><iframe src="{{ node.url }}"
        {%- if node.hasOption('width') %} width="{{ node.option('width') }}"{% endif -%}
        {%- if node.hasOption('height') %} height="{{ node.option('height') }}"{% endif -%}
        {%- if node.hasOption('title') %} title="{{ node.option('title') }}"{% endif -%}
        {%- if node.hasOption('allow') %} allow="{{ node.option('allow') }}"{% endif -%}
        {%- if node.hasOption('allowfullscreen') and node.option('allowfullscreen') %} allowfullscreen{% endif -%}
        ></iframe></sds-embed
>
{%- endif -%}
